<!DOCTYPE softwarelist SYSTEM "softwarelist.dtd">
<!--
license:CC0-1.0

    Roland SRX wave expansion boards, 32 MB each and word-wide, for the
    XV-3080, the XV-5080 and their relatives.  The board carries its own
    label in the clear at offset 0 of the scrambled image, "Roland SRX  "
    and then the magic 4F B0 58 A7 and the sixteen-byte identity the host
    compares, and the socket undoes eighteen permuted address lines within
    each 256 KB block and the eight data lines.

    Each entry is commented with that identity, with the name the machine
    shows and with the build date the board stamps ten bytes after it, all
    read from the descrambled image.  The year is that build date, which
    for the PG-SRX boards and for SRX-03 is the date of a rebuild and not
    of the product.

    Every image here is marked BAD_DUMP because no board has been read.
    Each is Roland's own wave data as their plugins carry it, where it is
    already descrambled, put back into board form by the inverse of the
    socket's permutation; the data is expected to be the board's, but
    nothing here testifies to that.

    A board's directory says what it brings.  SRX-02, SRX-10 and SRX-11
    carry patch banks as well as their wave and sample lists, and a host
    takes those patches; the rest of the images declare a wave list and a
    sample list and nothing more, so a host plays their waves and finds no
    patches on them.  Those three are also the three whose identity word is
    their own board number, where every image without patches has one from
    0x1f up, with the Roland Cloud re-issues.  That is a property of the
    plugin data these are made from, and a dump of a board may well not
    share it.

    The five PG-SRX images are Roland Cloud re-issues of boards whose
    numbers they do not carry.  Three are settled by their waves: PG-SRX
    Orchestra is SRX-06, whose waves are SR-JV80-02, -13 and -16; PG-SRX
    Keyboards is SRX-07, from SR-JV80-04, -08 and -10; PG-SRX World is
    SRX-09, from SR-JV80-05, -14 and -18.  Each one's wave names are found
    in those boards' images.  PG-SRX Strings and PG-SRX Classic EP have no
    SR-JV80 ancestor to match against; by their content they are likely
    SRX-04 and SRX-12.
-->

